1
00:00:32,283 --> 00:00:33,910
<i>What if I could put him in front of you?</i>

2
00:00:34,785 --> 00:00:36,621
<i>The man that ruined your life?</i>

3
00:00:37,872 --> 00:00:40,625
<i>If I could guarantee
that you'd get away with it</i>

4
00:00:41,417 --> 00:00:42,501
<i>would you kill him?</i>

5
00:03:26,290 --> 00:03:27,333
The Blackmore Cross.

6
00:03:27,458 --> 00:03:28,667
I understand it's your second.

7
00:03:28,751 --> 00:03:29,794
Congratulations.

8
00:03:30,252 --> 00:03:31,462
You have served honorably.

9
00:03:34,382 --> 00:03:35,883
So that's it?

10
00:03:36,425 --> 00:03:38,552
The order from the board
will come through shortly.

11
00:03:38,844 --> 00:03:39,970
The final order?

12
00:03:40,304 --> 00:03:41,305
Yes.

13
00:03:41,514 --> 00:03:42,765
And the Bomber?

14
00:03:45,017 --> 00:03:46,477
It's not your problem anymore.

15
00:03:47,436 --> 00:03:48,729
I don't need to remind you

16
00:03:48,813 --> 00:03:50,815
of the critical nature of your final mission.

17
00:03:51,482 --> 00:03:53,651
So please rest.

18
00:03:58,114 --> 00:04:00,074
<i>Every suspect educates us.</i>

19
00:04:01,325 --> 00:04:02,993
<i>Makes us better at what we do.</i>

20
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:07,164
<i>No more than this elusive sociopath.</i>

21
00:04:07,998 --> 00:04:08,999
<i>He's efficient.</i>

22
00:04:09,417 --> 00:04:11,335
<i>Precise with his timings.</i>

23
00:04:12,336 --> 00:04:14,964
<i>The press have dubbed
him the Fizzle Bomber.</i>

24
00:04:16,048 --> 00:04:17,633
<i>We stopped him this time.</i>

25
00:04:18,342 --> 00:04:20,636
<i>But this was only a small attack.</i>

26
00:04:23,973 --> 00:04:26,016
<i>This is his grand plan.</i>

27
00:04:27,184 --> 00:04:29,770
<i>March 1975.</i>

28
00:04:29,854 --> 00:04:31,439
<i>He keeps changing the day.</i>

29
00:04:33,441 --> 00:04:36,694
<i>The explosion will level
10 blocks of New York</i>

30
00:04:37,278 --> 00:04:40,030
<i>and leave over 11,000 dead.</i>

31
00:04:41,490 --> 00:04:43,784
<i>Everything we've done
to stop him has failed.</i>

32
00:04:44,702 --> 00:04:46,328
<i>But I was close this time.</i>

33
00:04:47,371 --> 00:04:49,540
Just one more try.

34
00:04:50,332 --> 00:04:51,709
<i>Grafts have taken.</i>

35
00:04:52,042 --> 00:04:54,712
The reconstructive transplant is stable.

36
00:04:55,463 --> 00:04:56,672
But you understand

37
00:04:57,131 --> 00:05:00,050
you will look different to what you remember.

38
00:05:01,093 --> 00:05:02,511
Eyes will heal.

39
00:05:03,220 --> 00:05:05,806
You should recover
well for this final mission.

40
00:05:06,474 --> 00:05:08,434
And then I'll be decommissioned.

41
00:05:09,393 --> 00:05:10,978
It's not just the physical injuries.

42
00:05:11,812 --> 00:05:13,647
You've logged more field hours

43
00:05:13,731 --> 00:05:16,108
than any of the other agents
I've been assigned to.

44
00:05:16,775 --> 00:05:18,068
The risks are real.

45
00:05:20,404 --> 00:05:22,573
The scars have begun to heal nicely.

46
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:29,246
Your vocal cord damage has reduced

47
00:05:29,830 --> 00:05:33,125
but will not regrow exactly the same way.

48
00:05:34,460 --> 00:05:36,545
There goes my singing career.

49
00:05:41,884 --> 00:05:43,594
It will take some getting used to.

50
00:05:55,564 --> 00:05:57,149
I've changed so much

51
00:05:58,817 --> 00:06:01,403
that I doubt
my own mother would recognize me.

52
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:15,042
The order finally came through today.

53
00:06:15,960 --> 00:06:17,169
I guess

54
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:19,129
it was inevitable.

55
00:06:20,756 --> 00:06:23,300
<i>By the time you listen
to this seven years will have passed.</i>

56
00:06:24,718 --> 00:06:26,095
<i>Our first mission</i>

57
00:06:26,178 --> 00:06:28,681
<i>is just as important as our last.</i>

58
00:06:28,973 --> 00:06:31,976
<i>Each one getting us
closer to our final destination.</i>

59
00:06:40,276 --> 00:06:41,944
Sir, please raise your right hand.

60
00:06:43,487 --> 00:06:45,698
Do you solemnly swear
to uphold the rules and regulations

61
00:06:45,781 --> 00:06:48,826
- set forth by T.B.R. Code 7286?
- I do.

62
00:06:48,993 --> 00:06:51,120
Do you accept that any diversion
from your mission parameters

63
00:06:51,203 --> 00:06:53,956
will result in immediate court martial?
And if convicted

64
00:06:54,123 --> 00:06:55,541
death by lethal injection.

65
00:06:55,666 --> 00:06:56,667
I do.

66
00:06:57,501 --> 00:06:58,502
Thank you, sir.

67
00:06:58,919 --> 00:06:59,920
Thank you.

68
00:07:07,886 --> 00:07:10,014
<i>Time. It catches up with us all.</i>

69
00:07:11,890 --> 00:07:13,392
<i>Even those in our line of work.</i>

70
00:07:16,186 --> 00:07:18,022
<i>I guess you could say we're gifted.</i>

71
00:07:19,607 --> 00:07:22,526
<i>God, Jesus, it sounds arrogant
saying that out loud.</i>

72
00:07:23,694 --> 00:07:24,778
<i>All right.</i>

73
00:07:24,862 --> 00:07:26,196
<i>I'll put it a better way.</i>

74
00:07:27,698 --> 00:07:28,991
<i>I guess you could say</i>

75
00:07:29,867 --> 00:07:31,660
<i>we were born into this job.</i>

76
00:08:26,131 --> 00:08:27,716
Hey, look at this freak.

77
00:08:32,054 --> 00:08:33,263
There you go, Derek.

78
00:08:35,683 --> 00:08:36,725
What can I get you?

79
00:08:37,101 --> 00:08:38,102
Old Underwear.

80
00:08:38,769 --> 00:08:40,688
Old Underwear. Straight up?

81
00:08:46,568 --> 00:08:47,569
Leave the bottle.

82
00:08:49,905 --> 00:08:52,032
From the look of you
I'm guessing you're not celebrating.

83
00:08:52,533 --> 00:08:53,534
From the look of me?

84
00:08:54,118 --> 00:08:55,202
What do I look like?

85
00:08:58,163 --> 00:09:00,290
Just making conversation. Jesus.

86
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:03,961
<i>He's down!</i>

87
00:09:06,588 --> 00:09:07,589
Hey, bud, two more.

88
00:09:08,132 --> 00:09:09,341
Chaser, right?

89
00:09:16,724 --> 00:09:17,725
You're new.

90
00:09:18,684 --> 00:09:20,310
Yeah, I've been working
here a couple of weeks.

91
00:09:20,811 --> 00:09:22,271
Yeah? How's it working out?

92
00:09:22,896 --> 00:09:23,981
It's been quiet.

93
00:09:24,690 --> 00:09:25,774
People staying home.

94
00:09:26,984 --> 00:09:28,819
Because of the big, bad Fizzle Bomber?

95
00:09:28,902 --> 00:09:29,903
Yeah, I guess.

96
00:09:31,029 --> 00:09:32,030
Love it.

97
00:09:32,698 --> 00:09:34,158
Like hiding makes you any safer.

98
00:09:34,867 --> 00:09:36,076
Doesn't scare you?

99
00:09:38,078 --> 00:09:40,330
Worrying about every freak show
on the subway won't change things.

100
00:09:40,622 --> 00:09:42,916
That is true.

101
00:09:43,792 --> 00:09:44,793
Haven't seen you around.

102
00:09:45,169 --> 00:09:46,170
You come here often?

103
00:09:46,295 --> 00:09:47,337
What are you, a faggot?

104
00:09:49,047 --> 00:09:50,215
What's your problem?

105
00:09:50,549 --> 00:09:51,550
Hey!

106
00:09:52,676 --> 00:09:54,011
I was joking man. Jesus.

107
00:09:54,094 --> 00:09:55,304
Yeah, well, that's not funny.

108
00:09:56,930 --> 00:09:58,015
Tell me a good one then.

109
00:09:58,390 --> 00:10:00,017
- What, a joke?
- Yeah, come on.

110
00:10:00,893 --> 00:10:02,269
You know, a free shot. Come on.

111
00:10:02,352 --> 00:10:03,353
No, no, I don't know any.

112
00:10:03,729 --> 00:10:06,106
You work in a bar for Christ sakes.
Surely you know a joke.

113
00:10:06,190 --> 00:10:08,609
No, I'm terrible with jokes.
Never remember them.

114
00:10:08,692 --> 00:10:09,693
One joke?

115
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:14,323
All right, I know one.

116
00:10:14,406 --> 00:10:15,407
But it's not funny.

117
00:10:15,991 --> 00:10:16,992
I'm all ears.

118
00:10:17,868 --> 00:10:20,078
All right, well, a guy walks into a bar, right?

119
00:10:21,705 --> 00:10:23,123
- Jesus, man.
- Just kidding. No, no, no.

120
00:10:23,373 --> 00:10:24,541
I got a good one. I got a good one.

121
00:10:25,417 --> 00:10:27,127
What comes first, the chicken or the egg?

122
00:10:28,420 --> 00:10:29,421
The rooster.

123
00:10:29,713 --> 00:10:31,840
Oh, shit, see? I'm terrible.

124
00:10:32,883 --> 00:10:34,051
That's the best you got?

125
00:10:34,301 --> 00:10:35,302
- Yeah, well...
- That's not funny.

126
00:10:35,385 --> 00:10:36,512
I told you it wasn't.

127
00:10:41,141 --> 00:10:42,309
You ever think about that, though?

128
00:10:42,810 --> 00:10:43,811
About what?

129
00:10:43,977 --> 00:10:45,521
You know, about what comes first.

130
00:10:47,231 --> 00:10:48,190
What do I care?

131
00:10:48,273 --> 00:10:49,733
- I got more important shit to worry about.
- All right.

132
00:10:49,817 --> 00:10:51,276
Well, you're a big deal, you know.

133
00:10:51,401 --> 00:10:52,945
You got important things to do, right?

134
00:10:53,403 --> 00:10:54,404
What do you do?

135
00:10:55,030 --> 00:10:56,114
Whatever I have to.

136
00:10:56,573 --> 00:10:58,742
Yeah, well it's tough
making a living, that's for sure.

137
00:10:59,451 --> 00:11:00,869
What do you know about tough?

138
00:11:03,705 --> 00:11:05,249
As much as the next guy, I guess.

139
00:11:06,792 --> 00:11:08,877
How about as much as an unmarried mother?

140
00:11:11,380 --> 00:11:12,422
What does that mean?

141
00:11:15,133 --> 00:11:17,761
Four cents a word, I write confession stories.

142
00:11:18,887 --> 00:11:20,889
"The Unmarried Mother" is my pen name.

143
00:11:22,307 --> 00:11:23,308
Are you serious?

144
00:11:24,184 --> 00:11:25,185
Yeah.

145
00:11:25,352 --> 00:11:26,770
Right. Right!

146
00:11:27,187 --> 00:11:28,272
How's business?

147
00:11:28,772 --> 00:11:30,816
Business is okay. I have a weekly column.

148
00:11:30,941 --> 00:11:32,651
I write them, they print them, I eat.

149
00:11:34,570 --> 00:11:35,571
Right?

150
00:11:36,780 --> 00:11:38,949
- What, you read my work?
- Yeah, yeah.

151
00:11:39,116 --> 00:11:40,200
Does that surprise you?

152
00:11:40,784 --> 00:11:42,244
- Yeah, a little.
- Why?

153
00:11:42,452 --> 00:11:43,787
You don't fit the demo.

154
00:11:44,121 --> 00:11:45,122
The demo?

155
00:11:45,455 --> 00:11:48,041
- The demographic.
- Right. Right.

156
00:11:48,125 --> 00:11:49,960
You know, broken women
who want to feel better

157
00:11:50,043 --> 00:11:51,753
about their pitiful lives by reading

158
00:11:52,045 --> 00:11:54,423
hard luck stories of heartbreak and betrayal

159
00:11:54,506 --> 00:11:55,966
spewed across the glossy pages

160
00:11:56,049 --> 00:11:57,759
of 25 cent pulp sludge.

161
00:11:59,303 --> 00:12:00,262
Yeah, exactly.

162
00:12:00,345 --> 00:12:02,598
Yeah, well you're being a little hard
on yourself, don't you think?

163
00:12:03,015 --> 00:12:04,016
It's garbage.

164
00:12:04,975 --> 00:12:07,394
I thought I had some talent
telling stories but who am I kidding?

165
00:12:07,936 --> 00:12:09,271
I mean, you tell me. You've read them.

166
00:12:10,314 --> 00:12:12,024
I have to admit I've had a few

167
00:12:12,399 --> 00:12:15,068
dark nights
when I've looked to you for a little

168
00:12:15,235 --> 00:12:16,987
insight into the feminine mind.

169
00:12:17,195 --> 00:12:19,865
You know, it seems to me
you've got a real hit on the woman's angle.

170
00:12:20,365 --> 00:12:21,992
- Woman's angle?
- Yeah.

171
00:12:23,201 --> 00:12:24,828
Yeah, I know the woman's angle.

172
00:12:25,746 --> 00:12:26,747
I should.

173
00:12:27,247 --> 00:12:29,875
Why, you married? You got sisters?

174
00:12:29,958 --> 00:12:31,460
You wouldn't believe me if I told you.

175
00:12:32,586 --> 00:12:34,463
The one thing that this job has taught me

176
00:12:34,546 --> 00:12:36,548
is that truth is stranger than fiction.

177
00:12:36,798 --> 00:12:38,550
I have heard some incredible shit.

178
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:40,260
Nothing astonishes me anymore.

179
00:12:40,385 --> 00:12:41,803
You don't know what incredible means.

180
00:12:41,887 --> 00:12:43,180
Yeah? Well, try me.

181
00:12:47,935 --> 00:12:49,061
Here you go. I got that.

182
00:12:57,110 --> 00:12:58,528
Forget it, man. I'm not telling you.

183
00:12:58,946 --> 00:13:01,031
You're scared it's not good enough.

184
00:13:01,114 --> 00:13:02,407
- You think that'll work?
- Yeah.

185
00:13:02,491 --> 00:13:03,492
What are we, 12?

186
00:13:04,701 --> 00:13:05,702
Come on.

187
00:13:09,081 --> 00:13:11,541
Bet you the rest of that bottle
I got the best story you ever heard.

188
00:13:14,211 --> 00:13:15,337
Bet you a full bottle.

189
00:13:23,845 --> 00:13:24,846
What do you say?

190
00:13:28,517 --> 00:13:29,518
If I lose?

191
00:13:29,935 --> 00:13:31,269
Just add a 20 to the tip.

192
00:13:33,981 --> 00:13:34,982
Why not?

193
00:13:37,317 --> 00:13:38,318
Hey, man.

194
00:13:38,568 --> 00:13:41,113
- Two Buds, bud.
- Right.

195
00:13:41,321 --> 00:13:43,031
<i>Mayor Davidson,
how do you respond to the fact</i>

196
00:13:43,115 --> 00:13:44,992
<i>that many New Yorkers are leaving the city</i>

197
00:13:45,075 --> 00:13:47,786
<i>in fear of a fifth attack by the Fizzle Bomber?</i>

198
00:13:47,869 --> 00:13:49,413
<i>We've got extra police patrols</i>

199
00:13:49,496 --> 00:13:51,456
<i>on 24 hour shifts around the city.</i>

200
00:13:51,915 --> 00:13:55,377
<i>We've created a joint task force
with the Federal Bomb Squad</i>

201
00:13:55,585 --> 00:13:58,922
<i>and the FBI in order
to track down the suspect.</i>

202
00:13:59,464 --> 00:14:00,924
What a stupid name.

203
00:14:01,049 --> 00:14:02,050
"Fizzle Bomber."

204
00:14:02,426 --> 00:14:04,761
The guy makes compressed
RDX-based explosive compounds.

205
00:14:05,053 --> 00:14:06,054
That's not easy.

206
00:14:06,221 --> 00:14:08,015
"Fizzle Bomber" is easier to remember.

207
00:14:08,348 --> 00:14:09,349
I hate that name.

208
00:14:09,891 --> 00:14:11,393
Makes it sound like his bombs just fizzle.

209
00:14:11,476 --> 00:14:12,644
Like they don't do any real damage.

210
00:14:13,270 --> 00:14:14,646
I don't think anybody's doubting

211
00:14:15,105 --> 00:14:16,898
how dangerous this asshole is.

212
00:14:17,107 --> 00:14:19,943
Well, there's a lot of selfish assholes
out there needing a good shake up.

213
00:14:20,986 --> 00:14:22,195
They should be afraid.

214
00:14:25,615 --> 00:14:27,784
Well, he's killed 112 people already.

215
00:14:28,326 --> 00:14:29,619
Some people just gotta go.

216
00:14:30,203 --> 00:14:32,289
Stops the gene pool right in its tracks.

217
00:14:32,539 --> 00:14:33,540
Yeah.

218
00:14:33,623 --> 00:14:35,292
Maybe he's doing the city a favor.

219
00:14:39,921 --> 00:14:41,590
Better be careful who hears you say that.

220
00:14:42,049 --> 00:14:43,133
What, you think I'm wrong?

221
00:14:43,467 --> 00:14:45,635
I'm just saying
that kind of talk can get you in trouble.

222
00:14:47,220 --> 00:14:48,805
No worse than I've already been through.

223
00:14:49,389 --> 00:14:52,059
Now listen,
you keep promising the best story of my life.

224
00:14:52,976 --> 00:14:54,269
But I'm not hearing anything.

225
00:15:02,360 --> 00:15:03,361
All right.

226
00:15:04,404 --> 00:15:05,530
Guess I'll start at the beginning.

227
00:15:10,660 --> 00:15:11,995
When I was a little girl...

228
00:15:12,913 --> 00:15:13,914
What?

229
00:15:15,874 --> 00:15:17,209
What, you want me to tell my story?

230
00:15:17,292 --> 00:15:18,502
No, I just, I thought...

231
00:15:20,003 --> 00:15:21,838
I'm sorry. Yeah, sure. Go on, go on.

232
00:15:27,552 --> 00:15:28,720
When I was a little girl...

233
00:15:35,352 --> 00:15:36,728
You ever hear of Christine Jorgensen?

234
00:15:37,229 --> 00:15:38,230
Roberta Cowell?

235
00:15:38,396 --> 00:15:40,107
Yeah, yeah, they had

236
00:15:40,273 --> 00:15:42,109
sex change.

237
00:15:43,360 --> 00:15:44,361
So you...

238
00:15:44,444 --> 00:15:45,445
Don't interrupt.

239
00:15:50,534 --> 00:15:53,453
It was September 13th, 1945.

240
00:15:56,581 --> 00:15:57,624
<i>I was a foundling.</i>

241
00:15:58,375 --> 00:16:00,502
<i>A newborn left on an orphanage doorstep.</i>

242
00:16:09,302 --> 00:16:10,720
City of Cleveland Orphanage.

243
00:16:19,271 --> 00:16:20,397
That's a good girl.

244
00:16:22,315 --> 00:16:24,609
Well, there's no signs of trauma or exposure.

245
00:16:25,026 --> 00:16:26,194
The child appears healthy.

246
00:16:27,404 --> 00:16:30,782
If she shows any signs
of infection or excessive coughing,

247
00:16:30,907 --> 00:16:33,160
she should be taken
to the hospital for a thorough examination.

248
00:16:33,243 --> 00:16:34,244
Yes, Doctor.

249
00:16:35,579 --> 00:16:36,621
She got a name?

250
00:16:38,123 --> 00:16:39,124
Jane.

251
00:16:39,666 --> 00:16:41,168
Jane will have to do for now.

252
00:16:41,668 --> 00:16:43,670
I'll be back to check on Jane in a week.

253
00:16:43,795 --> 00:16:45,088
I look forward to it.

254
00:16:50,677 --> 00:16:52,220
Don't worry, sweetheart.

255
00:16:52,679 --> 00:16:54,306
We'll take good care of you now.

256
00:16:59,102 --> 00:17:01,605
<i>I was just one
of a dozen babies who had no past.</i>

257
00:17:04,107 --> 00:17:05,609
<i>But that was all we had in common.</i>

258
00:17:07,944 --> 00:17:10,113
<i>I was never sick, not one day.</i>

259
00:17:10,197 --> 00:17:11,823
<i>So I was never taken to a hospital.</i>

260
00:17:12,616 --> 00:17:14,159
<i>Not until later in my life.</i>

261
00:17:30,675 --> 00:17:31,676
Slow down.

262
